WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.390
Spider-Man: Far From Home catches up with
your friendly neighborhood wall-crawler as

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:08.920
he heads out of Queens for a wide-ranging
field trip through the best touristy vistas

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:10.580
that Europe has to offer.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.870
But it's not all fun and games on this journey
abroad.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:18.080
Let's break it all down with some small details
you may have missed in the movie's first trailer.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.600
The first Far From Home trailer includes a
close-up shot of Peter Parker's passport,

00:00:22.609 --> 00:00:26.460
in which Tom Holland still somehow manages
to look like a movie star, in spite of an

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.300
intentionally goofy photo.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.170
While the passport lacks any specific years
on any of its date sections, it does include

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:36.539
a cute nod to Spider-Man's real-life comic
book origins.

00:00:36.539 --> 00:00:41.359
Pausing the video to take in all the details,
viewers may notice that Peter Parker's birthday

00:00:41.359 --> 00:00:45.839
is listed as August 10th, a seemingly inconsequential
bit of trivia.

00:00:45.839 --> 00:00:49.589
What you may not have realized is that August
10th really is the day that Spider-Man as

00:00:49.589 --> 00:00:54.369
we know him was born, with the character making
his first appearance in a comic book on that

00:00:54.369 --> 00:01:00.999
date, way back in 1962, in Amazing Fantasy
#15, written and penciled by Stan Lee and

00:01:00.999 --> 00:01:01.999
Steve Ditko.

00:01:01.999 --> 00:01:06.440
While the character's origins would be told
and retold many times over many comic issues

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:10.869
over the years to come, all the core details
are there in his initial appearance.

00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:15.189
Peter's now-canonical date of birth in the
MCU is a great tip of the hat to the day that

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.240
got this whole franchise started.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:22.320
Much of the first Far From Home trailer showcases
Peter making his way around Europe in a sleek

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.909
new suit, designed with red and black colors.

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:28.720
While it looks similar to the red-and-blue
suits he sported in Captain America: Civil

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.710
War and Spider-Man: Homecoming, the Far From
Home trailer's new suit seems to once again

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:38.479
pay tribute to Steve Ditko's original comic
book cover design, which seems to feature

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:41.109
a suit that's almost more black than blue.

00:01:41.109 --> 00:01:45.460
Even more interestingly, this isn't the only
change of clothes we see Spidey wear in the

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:46.460
trailer.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:50.689
Towards the end, we get a glimpse of a new,
stealthy looking black costume that Spider-Man

00:01:50.689 --> 00:01:52.820
seems to be wearing during a night mission.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:56.590
It's safe to assume it came from Nick Fury,
though we'll have to wait until we see the

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:57.590
movie to know for sure.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:01.759
Meanwhile, this can be seen as yet another
reference to a couple of now-classic Spidey

00:02:01.759 --> 00:02:06.219
looks from the comics: the Spider-Man Noir
suit, and — of course — Spider-Man's original

00:02:06.219 --> 00:02:07.040
black suit.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.159
In an act of mercy to moviegoers everywhere,
the MCU entries in the Spider-Man canon have

00:02:12.159 --> 00:02:16.349
so far spared us yet another death scene for
poor old Uncle Ben.

00:02:16.349 --> 00:02:21.200
Sure, sure, "With great power must also come
great responsibility," but we don't need another

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.840
origin story with Ben eating a street thug's
bullet to get the point anymore.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.440
While the character played a major part in
the Tobey Maguire and Andrew Garfield Spider-Man

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.830
movies, Peter's beloved uncle isn't expected
to make an onscreen appearance in the Tom

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:35.209
Holland movies.

00:02:35.209 --> 00:02:39.280
But despite his physical absence from the
franchise, his presence still looms large

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.040
over Peter Parker's story.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:46.670
In Spider-Man: Homecoming, Peter alluded to
a desire to protect Aunt May from undue, Spider-Man-related

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:47.670
stress:

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:48.670
"May cannot know.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:49.860
I cannot do that to her right now.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:50.860
You know?

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.280
I mean, everything that's happened with her,
I—please."

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.610
It's a great way to tell the story that so
many of us already know, letting viewers engage

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:01.030
with the movie by filling in the blanks themselves.

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:06.379
Far From Home takes this thread further with
the initials "BFP" stitched onto Peter's luggage,

00:03:06.379 --> 00:03:10.060
visible in the center of frame while he's
packing for his trip to Europe.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.519
These initials probably belong to Uncle Ben
F. Parker.

00:03:12.519 --> 00:03:17.390
It's a heartwarming, bittersweet touch to
include in the movie, implying that no matter

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:21.430
where Peter goes on his adventures, whether
it's across the pond to Europe or beyond the

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:25.840
stars to Titan, he'll always be carrying a
little bit of his Uncle Ben with him.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.200
Picking up where the last Spider-Man movie
left off, Far From Home's first trailer starts

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:35.510
with Spider-Man and Aunt May standing side-by-side
at a charitable benefit, with May having full

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:38.209
knowledge of the superhero's real-life identity.

00:03:38.209 --> 00:03:44.129
Then Happy Hogan, Tony Stark's loyal, consistently-harried
assistant, shows up to Spider-Man's charity

00:03:44.129 --> 00:03:48.920
fundraiser with a gigantic novelty check from
the Stark Relief Foundation, signed by none

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:50.810
other than...Pepper Potts.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:55.040
The sequence of events seems to imply that
whatever happens in Avengers: Endgame, Pepper

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.390
Potts will make it out alive - otherwise this
reference would come off as a little bit morbid.

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:03.010
One thing that's less clear is just exactly
what happened to Tony Stark.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:07.260
Iron Man was a pretty big part of Peter's
life in Spider-Man: Homecoming and Avengers:

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:11.781
Infinity War, and the presence of Happy and
Pepper with Tony nowhere in sight is a little

00:04:11.781 --> 00:04:12.900
bit worrisome.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:13.989
We don't want to draw conclusions.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.320
Has he at least made it back from space yet?

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:21.800
The Far From Home trailer includes glimpses
of several new bad guys, including a being

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:26.590
made out of water, a fiery monster, and a
titanic bruiser who appears to be made out

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:27.590
of sand.

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:31.690
And let's not forget the curiously-violent
lightning bolts flying around during an action

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:33.860
sequence at London's Tower Bridge.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:38.020
The enemies all appear to be reminiscent of
some key villains from Spider-Man's comic

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:41.310
past, including Molten Man, Sandman, Hydro-Man,
and, potentially, Electro.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:45.440
But are these monsters really classic Spidey
villains?

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:46.930
Or are they something different?

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:52.040
Well, there's one detail you might've missed
that could indicate the former over the latter...

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:56.840
While Peter's best friend Ned Leeds has his
picture taken in Venice, you can see a boat

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:02.190
in the background floating by with "Asm 212"
printed boldly on its side.

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:05.220
Did that combination of letters and numbers
tickle your Spidey-Sense?

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.160
If so, there's a pretty good reason for it.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.600
The text is a clear reference to the 212th
issue of ​Amazing Spider-Man​, published

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:18.100
in January 1981, featuring the very first
appearance of Morris Bench - the villain also

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.660
known as Hydro-Man.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:24.170
It's too soon to say if Far From Home​ features
that specific version of the character - or

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:25.890
if all these monsters are something else entirely.

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:30.040
But either way, this little code on the boat
is a nice nod to Spidey's past.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:34.360
The tail end of the Far From Home trailer
features the grand entrance of Jake Gyllenhaal

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:38.740
as Mysterio, making his debut on the silver
screen in a dramatic way.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:42.230
"You don't want any part of this"

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:46.310
While Mysterio has long been Spider-Man's
enemy in Marvel Comics, here he shows up to

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:50.130
fight as a hero — or, at least, that's how
things appear to be.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:54.630
In the comics, Mysterio - who's real name
is Quentin Beck - is a stunt man and special

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:59.060
effects expert for the movie business who
uses elaborate illusions to appear as though

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:02.070
he's wielding real magic, usually for the
sake of committing crimes.

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:05.800
It obviously begs the question - is this trailer
pulling a trick on us?

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:10.510
After Mysterio shows up to take over the fight
against the gigantic creature made of water,

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:16.010
his exploits end up on a news broadcast, crediting
the "mystery man" for fighting back the threat.

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:20.320
Sure, it's possible that Mysterio is just
here to help, but what if this special effects

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:24.680
wizard was using high-tech illusions to make
these fights happen, engineering himself a

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:28.680
heroic moment at the expense of Spider-Man
and countless civilians?

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:32.820
We can't tell for sure just yet, but the way
the fight works out for Quentin's benefit

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:35.940
is definitely a little... mysterious.

